In this lesson, we'll look at what happens when you repeat a chance experiment again and again. One coin toss doesn't tell you much, but run it many times and a pattern starts to appear. A single run is called a trial. Each time you toss the coin counts as one trial. The number of times your event happens, say heads, is the frequency. Divide that frequency by the total number of trials and you get the relative frequency, which is always a number between zero and one. Now here's the key idea. The relative frequency won't match the predicted probability perfectly straight away. Toss a coin ten times and you might get six heads, so a relative frequency of about zero point six, even though the prediction for a fair coin is zero point five. But keep going, run a thousand tosses, and that relative frequency usually settles much closer to one half. This is the law of large numbers in action. More trials, and your observed proportion drifts towards the theory. Now let's see it in action.